NEB's report confirms what ENGOs and researchers have been talking about for the last decade:

1. That low-carbon economies are robust and are no threat to GDP growth nor employment;
2. Carbon must cost >$100/t, preferrably >$200/t;
3. Stronger action and sharper gov. policies are needed to curb emissions;
4. Solar and Wind power are cheaper than fossil fuel sources. So why do our federal and provincial governements keep subsidizing fossil fuels through lax carbon taxes and cheap developmental permits?

And all this talk about CO2-emissions doesn't even touch on the health costs of hte pollution caused by our local sources of emissions (http://edition.cnn.com/2017/10/19/health/pollution-1-in-6-deaths-study/index.html)

